{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"assumptions_and_dependencies\":\"Positive natural numbers mean values ≥ 1. For the zero-impact comparison, a narrowed predicate would be `ForkFreePositive A := A ⊆ Set.Ici 1 ∧ ForkFree A`. Only the mounted repository bytes were used; the linked external source was not accessed.\",\"build_or_check_outcome\":\"Textual and semantic analysis only. The current theorem contains a non-`sorry` proof, but no independent Lean check was possible: `lake env lean /tmp/FIVE03.lean` returned `/bin/bash: lake: command not found`. No repository files were changed; final `git status --short` was empty.\",\"case_id\":\"FIVE-03\",\"evidence\":\"`git rev-parse HEAD` → `9cbe1d3c12998c786b7c2cd99ce28a21b6631f66`.\\n`git hash-object FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean` → `3f7ccf1a845ef966b2dfbcb11918f88308c336bb`; comparison with that blob succeeded.\\n`sha256sum FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean` → `ee47c66fe29bcdd5e6bde2d8c325fcf8a511f5a147f8cef8c1065cd28716fae3`.\\n`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean:30-33`: `ForkFree` imposes only the divisibility/subsingleton condition; it does not require positivity.\\nGeneral zero counterexample: `ForkFree {0,1,2}` is false because for `a=1`, the distinct other elements `0` and `2` are both divisible by `1`; `ForkFree {1,2}` is true. Also, `ForkFree {0}` is true, confirming that positivity is not enforced.\\n`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean:37-39`: every witness for `f n` satisfies `A ⊆ Set.Icc 1 n`, hence every member is ≥1. Therefore replacing `ForkFree` by `ForkFreePositive` yields the same admissible witnesses and the same `f`, including when `n=0`.\\n`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean:50`: the lower-bound docstring claims `[⌊n/3⌋, n]` is fork-free.\\n`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean:55-57`: the proof actually constructs `A = Finset.Icc (n / 3 + 1) n`.\\nThe claimed interval is false at `n=3`: `[⌊3/3⌋,3]={1,2,3}` is not fork-free because `1` divides the distinct other elements `2` and `3`. The constructed interval `{2,3}` is fork-free.\\n`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean:52-72`: zero is absent from the constructed set, so allowing zero in the general helper does not affect the lower-bound theorem's proposition or construction.\",\"limitations\":\"This does not establish fidelity to the external erdosproblems.com page, which was unavailable under the no-network constraint. It establishes an internal docstring/proof mismatch in the exact requested blob. Narrowing `ForkFree` would leave `f` extensionally unchanged, but the existing proof bytes would require an added positivity obligation; no such definition change is proposed.\",\"proposed_result\":\"Make one metadata-only wording correction in `FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/1062.lean`: replace the lower-bound docstring’s interval `[⌊n/3⌋, n]` with `[⌊n/3⌋ + 1, n]`. This matches the exact construction `Finset.Icc (n / 3 + 1) n` and avoids the counterexample at `n=3`. Do not change `ForkFree`, `f`, or either theorem proposition. The zero discrepancy is a broader helper-domain choice, not a source-definition error for `f`.\",\"question\":\"What is the smallest truthful correction supported by the exact FIVE-03 bytes, and does admitting zero affect `ForkFree`, `f`, or the lower-bound theorem?\",\"result_status\":\"statement_correction\",\"result_type\":\"proposed_result\",\"smallest_independent_check\":\"Read lines 50 and 55-57, then set `n=3`: the documented interval is `{1,2,3}`, where `1` divides both `2` and `3`, while the proof uses `{2,3}`. This independently verifies the one-character-boundary correction without relying on external sources.\"}"}}
